    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d807">

                <pb facs="0765" n="757" xml:id="pg757"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/12683: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-07-01T19:00:00+02:00">July 1, 1941—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 1—12:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2765. Department’s 2276, June 24, 8 p.m.,<anchor n="20" xml:id="d807-f1"/> was
                    communicated informally to the Foreign Office with the comments suggested and a
                    letter has been received from Sir Alexander Cadogan<anchor n="21"
                        xml:id="d807-f2"/> which concludes with the following paragraph: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“I am sure that our military authorities in the Middle East are already
                            taking all possible steps compatible with the pursuit of military
                            operations to minimize civilian sufferings, but the offer of the United
                            States Government is being brought to their attention.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
